{
  "raw": {
    "https://www.notredame.edu.au/about-us/jobs-at-unda/employment-opportunities/job-details/_nocache?jobid=orr6zfwh": {
      "title": "EOI - Academic Sessional (Foundation Years Group Tutor), School of Medicine",
      "description": "Connect on social media Connect with Notre Dame on Social Media Australia LinkedIn X YouTube TikTok Fremantle Facebook Instagram Broome Facebook Instagram Sydney Facebook Instagram EOI - Academic Sessional (Foundation Years Group Tutor), School of Medicine APPLY How to Apply Position Description Need help? Ask Notre Dame For further information or enquiries, please contact People and Culture: jobs@nd.edu.au Employment Type Sessional Category Casual/ Sessional Location Sydney Department School of Medicine Share LinkedIn Facebook X Email EOI - Academic Sessional (Foundation Years Group Tutor), School of Medicine EOI – Academic Sessional ( Foundation Years Group Tutor ) S chool of Medicine Based on our Sydney Campus Friendly and collaborative work environment Rewarding opportunity offering career development About the university We believe in the power of education to transform lives, strengthen communities and help people become the best version of themselves. The University of Notre Dame Australia is a modern, national Catholic university that serves more than 12,000 students across its campuses in Fremantle, Broome and Sydney. With a community of over 1,000 academic and professional staff, Notre Dame is recognised for its personalised learning environment, strong graduate outcomes and holistic educational philosophy rooted in critical thinking, ethics and the Catholic intellectual tradition. About the role The National School of Medicine seeks to appoint Lecturers and Senior Lecturers in medicine to undertake the role of Group Tutors in the Foundation Years of the Doctor of Medicine program based at the Darlinghurst Site of the National School of Medicine, Sydney. These include Problem-Based Learning (PBL) Tutoring, Communication and Clinical Skills sessions, Clinical Debriefing sessions and other learning opportunities. Key Responsibilities Facilitate the following sessions in each week of the academic year with the allocated group of students: - problem based learning tutorials - clinical debriefing tutorial - clinical and communication skills training sessions; Participate in assessment and evaluation activities including marking and providing feedback in collaboration with the Assessments team. Attend meetings and professional development sessions as required by the Head of Foundation Years and the School. Participate in performance development activities as required by the school. Consult with students in relation to readings, weekly topics and assessment. Provide a high level of pastoral care to students Qualifications, skills & experience Possession of a Medical qualification registrable with AHPRA and a Fellowship of a relevant professional body or a PhD in a relevant discipline. Experience in teaching at master's level or higher or in medical, health or professional practice-based education settings. High level of interest in medical education and experience in teaching in small group tutorials. Keen interest in maintaining currency in scholarship and professional practice. Ability to, or a willingness to learn how to incorporate technology (e.g. Blackboard, Zoom, email, internet) into teaching and learning. Ability to deliver quality learning and teaching practices in multiple modes including online delivery. Ability to assess priority of tasks, manage workload and meet deadlines, in a busy education environment. Evidence of a high level of pastoral care to students. Benefits Fitness Passport on offer . Generous training and development opportunities. Friendly and collaborative working environment. How to apply If this sounds like you, please email a cover letter and your CV. Please specify your area of interest and subject matter expertise, as well as your availability, in your cover letter. We are looking for Academic Sessionals on an ongoing basis. We will be in contact should a role become available. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people are encouraged to apply. Applicants are expected to have current and valid work rights in Australia and will need to comply with mandatory check requirements. The University of Notre Dame Australia seeks to increase the diversity of our workforce to better meet the different needs of the University and its stakeholders and to improve equal opportunity outcomes for our staff. Enquiries about the role: Please contact Sharon Abraham sharon.abraham@nd.edu.au Back to current openings"
    },
    "https://www.notredame.edu.au/about-us/jobs-at-unda/employment-opportunities/job-details/_nocache?jobid=otA3yfwo": {
      "title": "EOI - Academic Sessional (Computer Science), School of Arts and Sciences",
      "description": "Connect on social media Connect with Notre Dame on Social Media Australia LinkedIn X YouTube TikTok Fremantle Facebook Instagram Broome Facebook Instagram Sydney Facebook Instagram EOI - Academic Sessional (Computer Science), School of Arts and Sciences APPLY How to Apply Need help? Ask Notre Dame For further information or enquiries, please contact People and Culture: jobs@nd.edu.au Employment Type Sessional Category Casual/ Sessional Location Australia Department School of Arts & Sciences Share LinkedIn Facebook X Email EOI - Academic Sessional (Computer Science), School of Arts and Sciences EOI - Academic Sessional (Computer Science) , School of Arts & Sciences Fremantle and Sydney Campus Rewarding opportunity Friendly and collaborative environment Ongoing learning and professional development opportunitie s About the university We believe in the power of education to transform lives, strengthen communities and help people become the best version of themselves. The University of Notre Dame Australia is a modern, national Catholic university that serves more than 12,000 students across its campuses in Fremantle, Broome and Sydney. With a community of over 1,000 academic and professional staff, Notre Dame is recognised for its personalised learning environment, strong graduate outcomes and holistic educational philosophy rooted in critical thinking, ethics and the Catholic intellectual tradition. About the school The National School of Arts and Sciences is one of two National Schools in the Faculty of Arts, Sciences, Law and Business. The National School is based on two distinct campuses in Fremantle and Sydney. The School of Arts and Sciences delivers a practical, ethical and industry-focused education in diverse areas of study such as the liberal arts, communications and media, counselling, psychology, social work, and science, with a range of undergraduate and postgraduate programs on offer. About the role An academic sessional makes a substantial contribution to teaching programs at both the undergraduate and postgraduate levels. The position requires the preparation and coordination of lectures, tutorials, and workshops , consultation with students , assessment marking , and attendance at meetings as required by the relevant Course Coordinators and the Computer Science Discipline Head. The primary purpose of an academic sessional is to successfully deliver and manage their assigned courses within the discipline area. The school seeks expressions of interest from experienced academics who have a passion for teaching. Teaching opportunities are available on a sessional basis in the Bachelor of Computer Science and Master of Computer Science programs. We are looking for academic sessional staff with subject matter expertise in three or more of the following areas: Operating Systems Computer Architecture Computational Modelling Programming Languages and Compilers Computer Systems and Networks Artificial Intelligence Cyber Security Industry- a ligned Projects . Key Responsibilities Teach foundational and advanced computer science courses in consultation with the Discipline Head. Actively contribute to the design and development of curriculum, incorporating contemporary advancements in the computer science discipline. Provide consultation to students regarding weekly learning activities and assessments. Mark assessments and provide feedback as outlined by the Course Coordinators. Attend meetings as required by the Course Coordinator or Discipline Head. Qualifications, skills & experience Possession of a doctoral qualification in computer science, or equivalent accreditation and/or standing. Knowledge and understanding of emerging technologies in computer science and related areas. Demonstrated ability to work effectively with individuals from diverse backgrounds. Ability to deliver high-quality learning and teaching practices in multiple modes, including face-to-face and hybrid delivery. Excellent interpersonal and communication skills, with the ability to foster collegiality and empathize with students. Proven ability to prioritize tasks, manage workload, and meet deadlines in a busy educational environment. Teaching experience, including the ability to develop and deliver computer science courses at undergraduate and postgraduate levels, incorporating new technologies and innovative approaches to teaching and learning. Strong networking skills and the ability to support the national and international positioning of the school in computer science or related disciplines. Previous experience working in the tertiary education sector is desirable. How to apply Please apply online with a resume and cover letter outlining your suitability for the role. Please indicate your area of interest and subject matter expertise in your cover letter. This is an Expression of Interest, and you will be contacted when the need arises. For further information on how to apply, please visit: How to apply | Notre Dame Applicants are expected to have current and valid work rights in Australia. The University is committed to providing a safe, respectful and inclusive working and learning environment. The successful candidate will be required to complete a self-declaration regarding Gender-Based Violence (as required by the National Code) as part of the pre-employment process. For more information on this please refer to our website. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people are encouraged to apply. The University of Notre Dame Australia seeks to increase the diversity of our workforce to better meet the different needs of the University and its stakeholders and to improve equal opportunity outcomes for our staff. Contact us Please direct your queries regarding this role to Tanveer Zia , Tanveer.Zia@nd.edu.au Unsolicited contact from recruitment agencies will not be entertained. Back to current openings"
    },
    "https://www.notredame.edu.au/about-us/jobs-at-unda/employment-opportunities/job-details/_nocache?jobid=ofd8zfwT": {
      "title": "EOI- Academic Sessional (Occupational Therapy), School of Health Sciences",
      "description": "Connect on social media Connect with Notre Dame on Social Media Australia LinkedIn X YouTube TikTok Fremantle Facebook Instagram Broome Facebook Instagram Sydney Facebook Instagram EOI- Academic Sessional (Occupational Therapy), School of Health Sciences APPLY How to Apply Need help? Ask Notre Dame For further information or enquiries, please contact People and Culture: jobs@nd.edu.au Employment Type Sessional Category Casual/ Sessional Location Sydney Department School of Health Sciences Share LinkedIn Facebook X Email EOI- Academic Sessional (Occupational Therapy), School of Health Sciences Campus: Sydney (Broadway), School of Health Sciences Rewarding opportunity Friendly and collaborative environment Flexible working arrangements Ongoing learning and professional development opportunities About the university We believe in the power of education to transform lives, strengthen communities and help people become the best version of themselves. The University of Notre Dame Australia is a modern, national Catholic university that serves more than 12,000 students across its campuses in Fremantle, Broome and Sydney. With a community of over 1,000 academic and professional staff, Notre Dame is recognised for its personalised learning environment, strong graduate outcomes and holistic educational philosophy rooted in critical thinking, ethics and the Catholic intellectual tradition . About the school The School of Health Sciences offers an extensive range of quality accredited undergraduate and postgraduate programs designed to engage students from learning opportunities in class through to professional workplace experience. Our Occupational Therapy program is distinctive for its teaching culture, delivery of quality contemporary education and its pastoral care mission. Our courses are delivered by experienced clinicians and academics ensuring materials and content are evidence based, contemporary and future focused. About the role An academic sessional makes a substantial contribution to the teaching program, particularly at the undergraduate level. The position requires preparation and coordination of tutorials and workshops, consultation with students, assessment marking and attendance at meetings as required by the Course Coordinator/Program Coordinator. The primary purpose of an academic sessional is to successfully run and manage their assigned tutorials or workshops within the discipline area. The School seeks expressions of interest from experienced Academics or Clinicians who have a passion for teaching. Teaching opportunities are available on a sessional basis in the Bachelor of Occupational Therapy Program. We are looking for academic sessional staff with subject matter expertise in one or more of the following occupational therapy practice areas: Rural, remote or international health and well-being Human pathophysiology or clinical medicine Paediatrics/school system Inpatient or Community Rehabilitation (aged care/disability/physical and cognitive) Key Responsibilities Conduct lectures/theoretical tutorials and/or workshops in consultation with the Discipline Lead. This includes preparing a brief lesson plan in line with the weekly learning objectives and facilitating informed group discussions relating to the content. Consultation with students in relation to readings, weekly topics and assessment. Marking and providing feedback relating to assessment activities as set out by the Course Coordinator/Program Coordinator. Attendance at meetings as required by the Course Coordinator/Program Coordinator. Manage short-term extension requests in line with University policy. Other duties as identified by the line manager or the National Deputy Head of School Comply with policies and procedures in all aspects of their work and conduct. About you Conduct lectures/theoretical tutorials and/or workshops in consultation with the Discipline Lead. This includes preparing a brief lesson plan in line with the weekly learning objectives and facilitating informed group discussions relating to the content. Consultation with students in relation to readings, weekly topics and assessment. Marking and providing feedback relating to assessment activities as set out by the Course Coordinator/Program Coordinator. Attendance at meetings as required by the Course Coordinator/Program Coordinator. Manage short-term extension requests in line with University policy. Other duties as identified by the line manager or the National Deputy Head of School Comply with policies and procedures in all aspects of their work and conduct. How to apply Please apply online with a resume and cover letter outlining your suitability for the role. Please specify your area of interest and subject matter expertise, as well as your availability, in your cover letter. We are looking for Academic Sessionals on an ongoing basis. For further information on how to apply, please visit: How to apply | Notre Dame Applicants are expected to have current and valid work rights in Australia. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people are encouraged to apply. The University is committed to providing a safe, respectful and inclusive working and learning environment. The successful candidate will be required to complete a self-declaration regarding Gender-Based Violence (as required by the National Code) as part of the pre-employment process. For more information on this please refer to our website. The University of Notre Dame Australia seeks to increase the diversity of our workforce to better meet the different needs of the University and its stakeholders and to improve equal opportunity outcomes for our staff. Contact us Please direct your queries regarding this role to Joanne Lewis , Joanne.Lewis@nd.edu.au Unsolicited contact from recruitment agencies will not be entertained. Back to current openings"
    },
    "https://www.notredame.edu.au/about-us/jobs-at-unda/employment-opportunities/job-details/_nocache?jobid=ozpGhfwF": {
      "title": "EOI - Academic Sessional, School of Medicine",
      "description": "Connect on social media Connect with Notre Dame on Social Media Australia LinkedIn X YouTube TikTok Fremantle Facebook Instagram Broome Facebook Instagram Sydney Facebook Instagram EOI - Academic Sessional, School of Medicine APPLY How to Apply Need help? Ask Notre Dame For further information or enquiries, please contact People and Culture: jobs@nd.edu.au Employment Type Sessional Category Casual/ Sessional Location Fremantle Department School of Medicine Share LinkedIn Facebook X Email EOI - Academic Sessional, School of Medicine EOI- Academic Sessional, School of Medicine Fremantle Campus Rewarding opportunity Friendly and collaborative environment Ongoing learning and professional development opportunities About the university The University of Notre Dame Australia is a private Catholic University with over 1,000 permanent staff providing an exceptional educational experience to over 12,000 students across our Fremantle, Broome, and Sydney campuses, as well as clinical schools in Victoria an d New South Wales. About the role The School seeks expressions of interest from registered medical practitioners and professionals in related fields who have a passion for teaching. Teaching opportunities are available in the Preclinical or Clinical Years of the Medical program and the Paramedicine program. These include; Problem-Based Learning (PBL) tutoring in Preclinical years; Clinical Debriefing (CD) tutoring in Preclinical and Clinical years, Clinical Skills tutoring and Research Training Pathway tutoring. Key Responsibilities Conduct teaching/tutorials/workshops/lectures to the standard of the Faculty . This may include preparation for the topic and facilitating group discussions relating to the content . Consultation with students in relation to learning and assessment . Participating in assessment activities, including marking and providing feedback . Attendance at meetings as required by the Course Coordinator . Other duties as identified by the line manager . Comply with policies and procedures in all aspects of their work and conduct, in particular the Staff Code of Conduct . Undertake Professional Development as arranged by the School . Qualifications, skills & experience Applicants applying for sessional positions with the Paramedicine program require registration with the Paramedicine Board of Australia, and a PG qualification in paramedicine and/or education | leadership. Hold a College Fellowship, a trainee position in a Fellowship Program or a PhD in a relevant area. Clinical Skills: A medical degree registrable with the Medical Board of Australia, plus at least one of the following: A College Fellowship or progress towards (e.g. FRACP, FRACGP, etc) and/or PhD in a field aligned with the research and teaching activities of the School ) . Clinical Debriefing : Hold a College Fellowship, a trainee position in a Fellowship Program or a PhD in a relevant area. Research Tutors : A PhD in a STEM discipline. Experience in collaborative-based learning environments . Skilled at facilitating small groups, discussions and evoking thoughts and questions from others . A high level of interest in medical education and experience in teaching in a health context . A keen interest in maintaining currency in scholarship and professional practice in the relevant discipline . Ability to, or a willingness to learn how to, incorporate technology into teaching and learning (e.g. Blackboard, zoom, email, internet) . Ability to deliver quality learning and teaching practices in multiple modes including online delivery . Excellent interpersonal and communication skills, including ability to work effectively in a team . Proven ability to assess priority of tasks, manage workload and meet deadlines, in a busy education environment . Evidence of a high level of pastoral care to students . Proven ability to work as an effective member of a team . Desirable: Experience working within a university or teaching environment . How to apply Please apply online with a resume and cover letter outlining your suitability for the role. Please address all selection criteria in in your cover letter. Please indicate in your cover letter which area you are interested in: Problem-Based Learning (PBL) tutoring in Preclinical years; Clinical Debriefing (CD) tutoring in Preclinical and Clinical years; Clinical Skills tutoring and Research Training Pathway tutoring Paramedicine Program For further information on how to apply, please visit: How to apply | Notre Dame This is an Expression of Interest only and you will be contacted when the need arises. Applicants are expected to have current and valid work rights in Australia. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people are encouraged to apply. The University of Notre Dame Australia seeks to increase the diversity of our workforce to better meet the different needs of the University and its stakeholders and to improve equal opportunity outcomes for our staff. Contact us Please direct your queries regarding this role to : Problem Based Learning : Uliya Gankande , uliya.gankande@nd.edu.au Clinical Skills : Uliya Gankande , uliya.gankande@nd.edu.au Research Tutors : Raoul Oehmen , raoul.oehmen@nd.edu.au Clinical Debriefing : Uliya Gankande , uliya.gankande@nd.edu.au Paramedicine: Joseph Cuthbertson, joseph.cuthbertson@nd.edu.au Unsolicited contact from recruitment agencies will not be entertained. Back to current openings"
    },
    "https://www.notredame.edu.au/about-us/jobs-at-unda/employment-opportunities/job-details/_nocache?jobid=oCVRAfwI": {
      "title": "EOI - Academic Sessional (Clinical Facilitator), School of Health Sciences",
      "description": "Connect on social media Connect with Notre Dame on Social Media Australia LinkedIn X YouTube TikTok Fremantle Facebook Instagram Broome Facebook Instagram Sydney Facebook Instagram EOI - Academic Sessional (Clinical Facilitator), School of Health Sciences APPLY How to Apply Need help? Ask Notre Dame For further information or enquiries, please contact People and Culture: jobs@nd.edu.au Employment Type Sessional Category Casual/ Sessional Location Sydney Department School of Health Sciences Share LinkedIn Facebook X Email EOI - Academic Sessional (Clinical Facilitator), School of Health Sciences EOI- Academic sessional (Clinical Facilitation Roles), Occupational T herapy National School of Health Sciences Based on our Sydney (Broadway) Campus Friendly and collaborative environment Rewarding opportunity Ongoing learning and professional development opportunities About the university We believe in the power of education to transform lives, strengthen communities and help people become the best version of themselves. The University of Notre Dame Australia is a modern, national Catholic university that serves more than 12,000 students across its campuses in Fremantle, Broome and Sydney. With a community of over 1,000 academic and professional staff, Notre Dame is recognised for its personalised learning environment, strong graduate outcomes and holistic educational philosophy rooted in critical thinking, ethics and the Catholic intellectual tradition . About the role The school seeks expressions of interest from experienced Academics or Clinicians who have a passion for teaching. We are looking for clinical facilitators for an ongoing basis. A Clinical Facilitator acts as a university representative to provide support and mentorship to students whilst out on clinical placements. The role involves periodic casual work supervising students across various facilities in Sydney. The program of Occupational Therapy (Sydney campus) within the National School of Health Sciences is calling out specifically for experienced Paediatric/ School based Occupational therapists, who will be available 1/day a week for 10-week blocks across the school terms. Key Responsibilities Supervise/support/mentor students whilst on placement . Identify students at risk, liaise with the clinical education team and the site supervisor to ensure appropriate supports and strategies are put into place . Providing both verbal and written feedback to the students about their performance . Completion of relevant professional development/supervision training through the university . Other duties as identified by the Director of Clinical Education. Qualifications, skills & experience AHPRA registered Occupational Therapist . Significant relevant clinical experience . Ability to deliver quality learning and teaching practices in multiple modes . Experience with the clinical supervision of students or relevant post-graduate studies . Demonstrated ability to deal effectively with individuals from diverse backgrounds . Knowledge of, and experience in adhering to workplace policies and procedures in the areas of work health safety, equity, diversity and promoting a respectful workplace culture . Excellent interpersonal and communication skills, including ability to foster high levels of collegiality and ability to empathise with students . How to apply Please apply online with a resume and cover letter outlining your suitability for the role. Please specify your area of interest and subject matter expertise, as well as your availability, in your cover letter. For further information on how to apply, please visit: How to apply | Notre Dame Applicants are expected to have current and valid work rights in Australia. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people are encouraged to apply. The University is committed to providing a safe, respectful and inclusive working and learning environment. The successful candidate will be required to complete a self-declaration regarding Gender-Based Violence (as required by the National Code) as part of the pre-employment process. For more information on this please refer to our website. The University of Notre Dame Australia seeks to increase the diversity of our workforce to better meet the different needs of the University and its stakeholders and to improve equal opportunity outcomes for our staff. Contact us Please direct your queries regarding the Clinical Facilitator role to Elaine Lee Elaine.lee@nd.edu.au Unsolicited contact from recruitment agencies will not be entertained. Back to current openings"
    },
    "https://www.notredame.edu.au/about-us/jobs-at-unda/employment-opportunities/job-details/_nocache?jobid=ohJxAfwR": {
      "title": "Student Support Officer, VET",
      "description": "Connect on social media Connect with Notre Dame on Social Media Australia LinkedIn X YouTube TikTok Fremantle Facebook Instagram Broome Facebook Instagram Sydney Facebook Instagram Student Support Officer, VET APPLY How to Apply Position Description Need help? Ask Notre Dame For further information or enquiries, please contact People and Culture: jobs@nd.edu.au Employment Type Part-Time Category Administrative Location Broome Department Vocational Education & Training Share LinkedIn Facebook X Email Student Support Officer, VET Student Support Officer, VET Broome Campus Part-time opportunity, (0. 8 ) Continuing c ontract Support VET administration and student services . Competitive Salary + 15% superannuation (pro-rata). About the university We believe in the power of education to transform lives, strengthen communities and help people become the best version of themselves. The University of Notre Dame Australia is a modern, national Catholic university that serves more than 12,000 students across its campuses in Fremantle, Broome and Sydney. With a community of over 1,000 academic and professional staff, Notre Dame is recognised for its personalised learning environment, strong graduate outcomes and holistic educational philosophy rooted in critical thinking, ethics and the Catholic intellectual tradition . About the office Vocational Education and Training (VET) at Notre Dame provides students with the skills they need to thrive in the education and health industries. We provide career preparation and formal qualifications to specialise in a certain area of work, to make a career change or as a pathway into higher education. Our programs are flexible and structured so students can learn online, on-campus and in the field. About the role The Student Support Officer provides administrative support to the VET team, helping coordinate student processes, support progression and act s as a key contact for students and stakeholders. T he position reports to the Operations Manager, Vocational Education and Training (VET). Key Responsibilities Support day-to-day administration, maintain student records, and assist with student course enquires . Maintain accurate student files and registers in line with compliance requirements. Respond to student and stakeholder enquiries. Support process improvements and comply with University policies . Keep program information current across relevant systems and portals. Qualifications, skills & experience Relevant degree or equivalent experience, education and training. Administrative experience in V ocational Education and Training or a R egistered Training Organisation environment, with knowledge of ASQA, training packages and government-funded programs. Strong Microsoft Office, LMS and CRM system skills. Excellent communication, customer service, organisation and time management skills. Ability to manage competing priorities, show attention to detail, solve problems and use initiative. Benefits Generous training and development opportunities. Friendly and collaborative working environment. Discounted study at the University. How to apply Please apply online with a resume and cover letter outlining your suitability for the role. Applications closing date: 13 October 2026 For further information on how to apply, please visit: How to apply | Notre Dame Applicants are expected to have current and valid work rights in Australia. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people are encouraged to apply. The University is committed to providing a safe, respectful and inclusive working and learning environment. The successful candidate will be required to complete a self-declaration regarding Gender-Based Violence (as required by the National Code) as part of the pre-employment process. For more information on this please refer to our website. The University of Notre Dame Australia seeks to increase the diversity of our workforce to better meet the different needs of the University and its stakeholders and to improve equal opportunity outcomes for our staff. Contact us Please direct your queries regarding this role to Marcia Rodriges , Marcia.Rodrigues@nd.edu.au Unsolicited contact from recruitment agencies will not be entertained. Back to current openings"
    },
    "https://www.notredame.edu.au/about-us/jobs-at-unda/employment-opportunities/job-details/_nocache?jobid=o6JxAfwG": {
      "title": "National Senior Student Support Officer - VET",
      "description": "Connect on social media Connect with Notre Dame on Social Media Australia LinkedIn X YouTube TikTok Fremantle Facebook Instagram Broome Facebook Instagram Sydney Facebook Instagram National Senior Student Support Officer - VET APPLY How to Apply Position Description Need help? Ask Notre Dame For further information or enquiries, please contact People and Culture: jobs@nd.edu.au Employment Type Full-Time Category Administrative Location Fremantle Department Vocational Education & Training Share LinkedIn Facebook X Email National Senior Student Support Officer - VET National Senior Student Support Officer , V ET Fremantle Campus Full-time opportunity, Fixed term c ontract ending August 2027 Support VET administration and student services. Competitive Salary + 15% superannuation . About the university We believe in the power of education to transform lives, strengthen communities and help people become the best version of themselves. The University of Notre Dame Australia is a modern, national Catholic u niversity that serves more than 12,000 students across its campuses in Fremantle, Broome and Sydney. With a community of over 1,000 academic and professional staff, Notre Dame is recognised for its personalised learning environment, strong graduate outcomes and holistic educational philosophy rooted in critical thinking, ethics and the Catholic intellectual tradition . About the office Vocational Education and Training (VET) at Notre Dame provides students with the skills they need to thrive in the education and health industries. We provide career preparation and formal qualifications to specialise in a certain area of work, to make a career change or as a pathway into higher education. Our programs are flexible and structured so students can learn online, on-campus a n d in the field. About the role The National Senior Student Support Officer coordinates administrative and student support activities across all VET programs, including program delivery, compliance, reporting and stakeholder engagement. Working closely with senior leaders, the position supports continuous improvement initiatives to enhance processes, service delivery and student outcomes. The position reports to the Operations Manager, Vocational Education and Training (VET). Key Responsibilities Coordinate VET administration while supporting team performance and positive student outcomes. Oversee records, compliance, reporting, events, projects and budgets. Resolve complex student and administrative matters. Drive process improvements to enhance service delivery and student outcomes. Build strong relationships with students, staff and external stakeholders. Qualifications, skills & experience Relevant degree with demonstrated experience, or an equivalent combination of education, training and professional experience. Proven experience leading administrative support and coordination, preferably within a VET, RTO or education environment. Experience in financial administration, procurement and project coordination. Ability to provide advice and support to senior leaders on administration, compliance, policy and process improvement. Strong administrative, project and process management skills, with excellent attention to detail and the ability to manage competing priorities. Strong analytical, problem-solving and initiative-taking skills. Excellent written and verbal communication skills, with the ability to engage and influence staff, students and external stakeholders. Demonstrated ability to improve administrative systems, processes and service delivery. Proficiency in Microsoft Office, with experience using LMS and CRM systems. Ability to lead, motivate and develop a team through guidance, mentoring and professional support. Knowledge of ASQA compliance, government-funded programs and VET operational requirements is highly desirable. Benefits Generous training and development opportunities. Friendly and collaborative working environment. Discounted study at the University. How to apply Please apply online with a resume and cover letter outlining your suitability for the role. Applications closing date: 13 October 2026 For further information on how to apply, please visit: How to apply | Notre Dame Applicants are expected to have current and valid work rights in Australia. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people are encouraged to apply. The University is committed to providing a safe, respectful and inclusive working and learning environment. The successful candidate will be required to complete a self-declaration regarding Gender-Based Violence (as required by the National Code) as part of the pre-employment process. For more information on this please refer to our website. The University of Notre Dame Australia seeks to increase the diversity of our workforce to better meet the different needs of the University and its stakeholders and to improve equal opportunity outcomes for our staff. Contact us Please direct your queries regarding this role to Marcia Rodrigues , Marcia.Rodrigues@nd.edu.au Unsolicited contact from recruitment agencies will not be entertained. Back to current openings"
    },
    "https://www.notredame.edu.au/about-us/jobs-at-unda/employment-opportunities/job-details/_nocache?jobid=oEJxAfwe": {
      "title": "Trainer and Assessor (School Based Education and Support), VET",
      "description": "Connect on social media Connect with Notre Dame on Social Media Australia LinkedIn X YouTube TikTok Fremantle Facebook Instagram Broome Facebook Instagram Sydney Facebook Instagram Trainer and Assessor (School Based Education and Support), VET APPLY How to Apply Position Description Need help? Ask Notre Dame For further information or enquiries, please contact People and Culture: jobs@nd.edu.au Employment Type Full-Time Category Education & Training Location Broome Department Vocational Education & Training Share LinkedIn Facebook X Email Trainer and Assessor (School Based Education and Support), VET Trainer and Assessor (School Based Education Support) VET Broome Campus Full-time opportunity, Continuing contract Join the University supporting its students and staff to achieve their career goals Be part of a welcoming community of professionals, academics and students Competitive Salary + 15% superannuation About the university The University of Notre Dame Australia is a private Catholic University with over 1,000 permanent staff providing an exceptional educational experience to over 12,000 students across our Fremantle, Broome, and Sydney campuses, as well as clinical schools in Victoria and New South Wales. About the office Vocational Education and Training (VET) at Notre Dame provides students with the skills they need to thrive in the education and health industries. We provide career preparation and formal qualifications to specialise in a certain area of work, to make a career change or as a pathway into higher education. Our programs are flexible and structured so students can learn online, on-campus and in the field. About the role The Trainer and Assessor will be responsible to plan, develop and deliver vocational education training and assessment in accordance with the relevant training packages, S tandards for Registered Training Organisations (RTOs) 2025 and the Australian Qualifications Framework. The Trainer and Assessor plays a crucial role in equipping our students with practical skills and knowledge that align with current industry requirements, thereby enhancing their employability and career progression. Key Responsibilities Training Delivery – Conduct training sessions, develop training materials, assessment methods adapt training methods and deliver y . Assessment and Evaluation – Assess learner competency, provide feedback, maintain records and participate in validation work processes. Learner Support – Support learners, monitor student progress and encourage development for a positive learning experience. Quality Assurance – Adhere to training standards, ensure continuous improvement, and compliance standards are achieved. Qualifications, skills & experience Essential Qualifications Relevant Degree or vocational qualification in School Based Education Support and / or Education / Teaching (primary or secondary) (Category A-D) . A Certificate IV in Training and Assessment - TAE40116 or TAE40122. S kills & E xperience In-depth knowledge and skills of the industry or sector in which training is delivered, including current trends, practices, and standards. Ongoing professional development in vocational education and competency-based training and assessment. Effective interpersonal and communication skills, with the ability to engage learners and stakeholders professionally. Ability to identify and address training or learner issues and implement appropriate strategies. Strong organisational skills, with experience in planning training sessions and managing documentation and compliance requirements. Demonstrated teamwork, problem-solving skills, and proficiency in Microsoft Office and relevant digital systems . Benefits On-site gym and fitness programs available. Located in historic Fremantle. Within walking distance from public transport. How to apply Please apply online with a resume and cover letter outlining your suitability for Applications closing date: 13 October 2026 For further information on how to apply, please visit: How to apply | Notre Dame Applicants are expected to have current and valid work rights in Australia. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people are encouraged to apply. The University of Notre Dame Australia seeks to increase the diversity of our workforce to better meet the different needs of the University and its stakeholders and to improve equal opportunity outcomes for our staff. Contact us Please direct your queries regarding this role to Marcia Rodrigues, Marcia.Rodrigues@nd.edu.au Unsolicited contact from recruitment agencies will not be entertained. Back to current openings"
    },
    "https://www.notredame.edu.au/about-us/jobs-at-unda/employment-opportunities/job-details/_nocache?jobid=oNWSzfwU": {
      "title": "Trainer and Assessor (Nursing), VET",
      "description": "Connect on social media Connect with Notre Dame on Social Media Australia LinkedIn X YouTube TikTok Fremantle Facebook Instagram Broome Facebook Instagram Sydney Facebook Instagram Trainer and Assessor (Nursing), VET APPLY How to Apply Position Description Need help? Ask Notre Dame For further information or enquiries, please contact People and Culture: jobs@nd.edu.au Employment Type Full-Time Category Education & Training Location Broome Department Vocational Education & Training Share LinkedIn Facebook X Email Trainer and Assessor (Nursing), VET Trainer and Assessor , (Nursing) V ET Broome campus Full-time opportunity, Continuing c ontract Join the University supporting its students and staff to achieve their career goals Be part of a welcoming community of professionals, academics and students Competitive Salary + 15% superannuation About the university The University of Notre Dame Australia is a private Catholic University with over 1,000 permanent staff providing an exceptional educational experience to over 12,000 students across our Fremantle, Broome, and Sydney campuses, as well as clinical schools in Victoria an d New South Wales. About the office Vocational Education and Training (VET) at Notre Dame provides students with the skills they need to thrive in the education and health industries. We provide career preparation and formal qualifications to specialise in a certain area of work, to make a career change or as a pathway into higher education. Our programs are flexible and structured so students can learn online, on-campus and in the field. About the role The Trainer and Assessor will be responsible to plan, develop and deliver vocational education training and assessment in accordance with the relevant training packages, Standards for Registered Training Organisations (RTOs) 20 25 and the Australian Qualifications Framework. The Trainer and Assessor plays a crucial role in equipping our students with practical skills and knowledge that align with current industry requirements, thereby enhancing their employability and career progression. Key Responsibilities Training Delivery – Conduct training sessions, develop training materials, assessment methods adapt training methods and deliver. Assessment and Evaluation – Assess learner competency, provide feedback, maintain records and participate in validation work processes. Learner Support – Support learners, monitor student progress and encourage development for a positive learning experience. Quality Assurance – Adhere to training standards, ensure continuous improvement, and compliance standards are achieved. Qualifications, skills & experience Essential requirements Hold a Bachelor of Nursing Qualification (Category A-C). Current registration as a Registered Nurse with the Nursing and Midwifery Board of Australia (NMBA) via the Australian Health Practitioner Regulation Agency (AHPRA). Hold a Certificate IV in Training and Assessment - TAE401 1 6 or TAE40122. Skills and Experience Strong industry knowledge and vocational competency relevant to the training area of nursing , including current standards and best practice. Ongoing professional development in vocational education and competency-based training and assessment. Effective interpersonal and communication skills, with the ability to engage learners and stakeholders professionally. Ability to identify and address training or learner issues and implement appropriate strategies. Strong organisation skills, with experience in planning training sessions and managing documentation and compliance requirements. Demonstrated teamwork, problem-solving skills, and proficiency in Microsoft Office and relevant digital systems . Benefits Collaborative environment with a passionate team dedicated to student success. Employee Family Study Discounts for further education at Notre Dame. Remote Allowance. How to apply Please apply online with a resume and cover letter outlining your suitability for the role. Applications closing date : 13 October 2026 For further information on how to apply, please visit: How to apply | Notre Dame Applicants are expected to have current and valid work rights in Australia. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people are encouraged to apply. The University of Notre Dame Australia seeks to increase the diversity of our workforce to better meet the different needs of the University and its stakeholders and to improve equal opportunity outcomes for our staff. The University is committed to providing a safe, respectful and inclusive working and learning environment. The successful candidate will be required to complete a self-declaration regarding Gender-Based Violence (as required by the National Code) as part of the pre-employment process. For more information on this please refer to our website. Contact us Please direct your queries regarding this role to Marcia Rodrigues , Marcia.Rodrigues@nd.edu.au Unsolicited contact from recruitment agencies will not be entertained. Back to current openings"
    },
    "https://www.notredame.edu.au/about-us/jobs-at-unda/employment-opportunities/job-details/_nocache?jobid=o2ESAfwS": {
      "title": "PhD Scholarship (Rural Healthcare and Intellectual Disability), School of Medicine",
      "description": "Connect on social media Connect with Notre Dame on Social Media Australia LinkedIn X YouTube TikTok Fremantle Facebook Instagram Broome Facebook Instagram Sydney Facebook Instagram PhD Scholarship (Rural Healthcare and Intellectual Disability), School of Medicine APPLY How to Apply Need help? Ask Notre Dame For further information or enquiries, please contact People and Culture: jobs@nd.edu.au Employment Type Full-Time Category Medicine Location Sydney Department School of Medicine Share LinkedIn Facebook X Email PhD Scholarship (Rural Healthcare and Intellectual Disability), School of Medicine The University of Notre Dame Australia is a Catholic university with campuses in Fremantle, Broome and Sydney. The Objects of the University are the provision of university education within a context of Catholic faith and values and the provision of an excellent standard of teaching, scholarship and research, training for the professions and the pastoral care of its students. PhD Scholarship, Full-Time, budgeted $42,440 for 3 years not indexed annually. Proposed start date January 2027. About the Scholarship This scholarship is based at the School of Medicine (Sydney campus) at the University of Notre Dame Australia. The applicant does not need to be on site. There is limited scope for the successful candidate to design their own research question, but it must be aligned with the wider project goals of examining the healthcare needs of rural people with intellectual disability as they grow older. It is well established that there are substantial inequalities in service provision for people with intellectual disability, particularly in non-metropolitan Australia. The complex needs of this population remain largely hidden and overlooked within the wider research agenda, leading to both inequality and inequity. The successful candidate would be working within an established research group including experienced disability researchers across a number of universities, other PhD candidates, disability support organisations and people with lived experience of intellectual disability. The project is also overseen by a dedicated project manager to support administrative process and day-to-day activities. The PhD scholarship will support the mixed method research component of the MRFF-funded project that will explore polypharmacy in older people with intellectual disability and include quantitative analysis of survey data and follow up interviews with older adults with intellectual disability and their carers - across regional and rural regions of Australia. Findings from this research will inform the development and evaluation of a nurse-led intervention in the project's final stages. The work is embedded within a co-designed, participatory research approach that prioritises lived experience and community partnership. Duration of the candidature Please note that the standard duration of candidature is 3 years full-time. If the extension of candidature is approved, the stipend will not be extended. Eligibility This opportunity is available for Domestic students only. Applicants are required to have completed a Bachelor’s degree with Honours (First Class or Upper Second Class) or a Masters by Research, in a relevant field of healthcare and be based in Sydney. Specific eligibility criteria for the PhD can be found here: Doctor of Philosophy (Medicine) | Notre Dame 2027 Applications Preference to commence Semester 1 2027. Deadline to submit your EOI is the 13st of November 2026. Why choose UNDA As a PhD candidate at Notre Dame University, you will embark on an intellectual journey guided by expert academic supervisors, working closely with them to shape, refine, and execute a significant research project that contributes original knowledge to the field. Enquiries Expressions of interest or enquiries related to the research topic, should be directed to Dr Giselle Gallego gisselle.gallego@nd.edu.au . Please submit the following documents: Previous academic transcripts and certificates. Research proposal outlining study objectives. Two letters of recommendation. CV/Resume. Any additional supporting documents. Back to current openings"
    },
    "https://www.notredame.edu.au/about-us/jobs-at-unda/employment-opportunities/job-details/_nocache?jobid=owt1vfwf": {
      "title": "EOI - Digital Learning and Design Intern",
      "description": "Connect on social media Connect with Notre Dame on Social Media Australia LinkedIn X YouTube TikTok Fremantle Facebook Instagram Broome Facebook Instagram Sydney Facebook Instagram EOI - Digital Learning and Design Intern APPLY How to Apply Position Description Need help? Ask Notre Dame For further information or enquiries, please contact People and Culture: jobs@nd.edu.au Employment Type General Casual Category EOI Location Australia Department Innovation & Design Share LinkedIn Facebook X Email EOI - Digital Learning and Design Intern EOI - D igital Learning and Design Inter ns, Innovation and Design Fremantle, Broome or Sydney campus Casual opportuni ties on offer - Open to Notre Dame current students only Casual opportunities on offer for enrolled students who have completed at least one semester or more of study at The University of Notre Dame Australia . Join the University supporting its students and staff to achieve their career goals ( L evel 3 ) WA/NSW payrate + 14 .5 % superannuation About the university The University of Notre Dame Australia is a private Catholic University with over 1,000 permanent staff providing an exceptional educational experience to over 12,000 students across our Fremantle, Broome, and Sydney campuses, as well as clinical schools in Victoria an d New South Wales. About the office/school The Innovation & Design Office is responsible for the transformation of learning and teaching at the University through learning innovations, with an emphasis on expert pedagogy, instructional design, quality and digital technologies to enhance the student learning journey and meet the University’s Objects and strategic direction as a Catholic university. About the role The position contributes to implementing and sustaining high-quality online, blended/hybrid and face-to-face practice across the University and participates in a team approach to meet the University’s Objects and strategic direction as a Catholic university. This position is open to current enrolled student students who have completed at least one semester or more of study at The University of Notre Dame Australia. Students must have full Australian work rights. We encourage all current eligible students to apply. Key Responsibilities Create eye-catching graphics and simple videos to make learning more engaging. Support the look and feel of online content across the university’s LMS. Collaborate with staff from different teams on real learning design projects. Contribute ideas in meetings and stay updated on new learning tools. Grow your skills through hands-on experience and training opportunities. Assist with other tasks as needed by your mentor or team. Represent the university professionally by following policies and showing initiative. Qualifications, skills & experience Essential Completed at least 1 semester or more of study at The University of Notre Dame Australia, with at least 1 semester of study remaining. Ability to work approximately 5 -10 hours per week. Proficient in Microsoft Office, with basic skills in graphic design or video editing (Canva/Adobe Creative Cloud). Excellent communication and interpersonal skills, with the ability to collaborate, seek guidance, and contribute to team efforts. Basic knowledge of digital tools and a strong interest in exploring modern technologies for teaching and learning. Basic knowledge of digital design principles and curiosity about current learning technologies. Strong time management skills with the ability to plan and track projects to meet deadlines, and a willingness to learn project management tools. Ability to work independently or as part of a team, support others, and learn from more experienced colleagues. Capable of assessing tasks, prioritising, and receiving feedback to address challenges. Willingness to adapt to new tools, workflows, and projects as needed. Desirable Participated in or helped coordinate a work-related project — such as organising a small even or improving a process. Has supported tasks that required organising information and identifying trends . Benefits Gain valuable hands-on experience in learning design whil st working with modern educational technologies . Friendly and collaborative working environment. How to apply Please apply online with a resume and cover letter outlining your suitability for the role . Also quote student number and duration of semester remaining until you graduate. Applications reviewed and interviewed when work is available. For further information on how to apply, please visit: How to apply | Notre Dame Applicants are expected to have current and valid work rights in Australia. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people are encouraged to apply. The University of Notre Dame Australia seeks to increase the diversity of our workforce to better meet the different needs of the University and its stakeholders and to improve equal opportunity outcomes for our staff. The University is committed to providing a safe, respectful and inclusive working and learning environment. The successful candidate will be required to complete a self-declaration regarding Gender-Based Violence (as required by the National Code) as part of the pre-employment process. For more information on this please refer to our website. Contact us Please direct general queries regarding this role to Michelle Katavatis, Head of Innovation and Design - michelle.katavatis@nd.edu.au Unsolicited contact from recruitment agencies will not be entertained. Back to current openings"
    },
    "https://www.notredame.edu.au/about-us/jobs-at-unda/employment-opportunities/job-details/_nocache?jobid=om7FAfws": {
      "title": "Student Recruitment Coordinator",
      "description": "Connect on social media Connect with Notre Dame on Social Media Australia LinkedIn X YouTube TikTok Fremantle Facebook Instagram Broome Facebook Instagram Sydney Facebook Instagram Student Recruitment Coordinator APPLY How to Apply Position Description Need help? Ask Notre Dame For further information or enquiries, please contact People and Culture: jobs@nd.edu.au Employment Type Full-Time Category Administrative Location Sydney Department Prospective Students Share LinkedIn Facebook X Email Student Recruitment Coordinator Student Recruitment Coor dinator, Prospective Students Office Oran Park campus Full-time opportunity, Fixed term c ontract end date mid-January 2028 Engage prospective students by providing expert advice on study options offered at the University. Join the university that takes a personalised approach to education. $ 106, 302 pa (level 6 ) + 15% superannuation . About the university We believe in the power of education to transform lives, strengthen communities and help people become the best version of themselves. The University of Notre Dame Australia is a modern, national Catholic university that serves more than 12,000 students across its campuses in Fremantle, Broome and Sydney. With a community of over 1,000 academic and professional staff, Notre Dame is recognised for its personalised learning environment, strong graduate outcomes and holistic educational philosophy rooted in critical thinking, ethics and the Catholic intellectual tradition . About the office The Prospective Students Office (PSO) is responsible for driving the University’s objectives in relation to the recruitment activities for domestic prospective students across all programs and supports the successful transition of strategy into operational plans across the entire student recruitment journey. About the role As a Student Recruitment Coordinator , you will build strong relationships with schools, career advisers, RTOs, and pathway providers, while delivering presentations, events, campus tours, and recruitment initiatives that inspire future students. You will guide prospective students through the application and enrolment process, manage enquiry and conversion activities, and work closely with Admissions, Marketing, Faculties, and Student Services to deliver an exceptional student experience. The position reports to the National Manager, Prospective Students Office. Key Responsibilities Drive domestic student recruitment and conversion activities. Build and manage relationships with schools, career advisers, RTOs, and pathway partners. Act as the key contact for assigned schools and develop tailored engagement plans. Coordinate school visits, presentations, campus tours, and recruitment events. Support Student Ambassador recruitment, training, and engagement. Manage enquiries and lead follow-up across multiple channels. Use recruitment and market data to inform engagement strategies. Develop and enhance recruitment materials and communications. Collaborate with Admissions, Marketing, Faculties, and Student Services teams. Maintain knowledge of University programs and admission requirements. Ensure compliance with University policies, branding, and privacy requirements. Qualifications, skills & experience As part of the selection process the preferred applicant will be required to obtain a valid Working with Children Check (WWCC), hold a current C-Class Driver’s License to travel within the metropolitan area and be available for after hours and weekend work as required. Relevant degree and/or equivalent experience. Proven student recruitment and student conversion experience. Understanding of the student recruitment lifecycle from enquiry to enrolment. Experience managing events, projects, or engagement activities. Experience using CRM systems and databases. Strong communication, presentation, and public speaking skills. Ability to analyse data and prepare reports. Proactive, results-focused, and able to take initiative. Excellent organisation, time management, and prioritisation skills. Ability to manage multiple tasks, adapt to changing priorities, and meet deadlines. Benefits Generous training and development opportunities. Friendly and collaborative working environment. Be part of a welcoming community of professionals, academics and students. How to apply Please apply online with a resume and cover letter outlining your suitability for the role. Applications closing date: 19 October 2026 For further information on how to apply, please visit: How to apply | Notre Dame Applicants are expected to have current and valid work rights in Australia. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people are encouraged to apply. The University is committed to providing a safe, respectful and inclusive working and learning environment. The successful candidate will be required to complete a self-declaration regarding Gender-Based Violence (as required by the National Code) as part of the pre-employment process. For more information on this please refer to our website. The University of Notre Dame Australia seeks to increase the diversity of our workforce to better meet the different needs of the University and its stakeholders and to improve equal opportunity outcomes for our staff. Contact us Please direct your queries regarding this role to Marcia Rodrigues , Marcia.Rodrigues@nd.edu.au Unsolicited contact from recruitment agencies will not be entertained. Back to current openings"
    },
    "https://www.notredame.edu.au/about-us/jobs-at-unda/employment-opportunities/job-details/_nocache?jobid=oEORAfwD": {
      "title": "Senior Lecturer (Medical Research and Innovation Domain), School of Medicine",
      "description": "Connect on social media Connect with Notre Dame on Social Media Australia LinkedIn X YouTube TikTok Fremantle Facebook Instagram Broome Facebook Instagram Sydney Facebook Instagram Senior Lecturer (Medical Research and Innovation Domain), School of Medicine APPLY How to Apply Position Description Need help? Ask Notre Dame For further information or enquiries, please contact People and Culture: jobs@nd.edu.au Employment Type Part-Time Category Medicine Location Sydney Department School of Medicine Share LinkedIn Facebook X Email Senior Lecturer (Medical Research and Innovation Domain), School of Medicine Senior Lecturer (Medical Research and Innovation Domain ) , School of Medicine Sydney Campus Part-time opportunity (0.5), Continuing contract Friendly and collaborative work environment Rewarding opportunity offering career development Level C from $147,864 + 15.00% superannuation About the university We believe in the power of education to transform lives, strengthen communities and help people become the best version of themselves. The University of Notre Dame Australia is a modern, national Catholic university that serves more than 12,000 students across its campuses in Fremantle, Broome and Sydney. With a community of over 1,000 academic and professional staff, Notre Dame is recognised for its personalised learning environment, strong graduate outcomes and holistic educational philosophy rooted in critical thinking, ethics and the Catholic intellectual tradition. About the role You will provide critical support to the Medical Research and Innovation Domain, assisting the Domain Lead to deliver the Sydney based Research Training Pathway across all four years of the MD program. Based at the Sydney campus, the role coordinates and supports student research and quality improvement projects from allocation through to completion, working collaboratively with academic and professional staff, clinical supervisors, the University’s Research Office, and Human Research Ethics Committee staff across NSW and Victoria. Key Responsibilities Coordinate Sydney Research Training Pathway delivery, project allocation, and milestone tracking. Maintain student project, supervision, and ethics records. Act as the key contact for project coordination and at-risk project escalation. Support ethics and governance approvals with relevant stakeholders. Recruit and support supervisors, ensuring MD-level project suitability. Collaborate with key stakeholders to embed MRI learning objectives across the MD Program. Drive continuous improvement of the Research Training Pathway. Support MRI teaching delivery and sessional educator coordination. Contribute to assessment design, marking, and examination activities. Participate in MRI teaching as required. Support scholarship, research, and medical education initiatives. Provide student support for project challenges and research stream transitions. Qualifications, skills & experience Possession of : a PhD or a Medical qualification registrable with AHPRA and a Fellowship of a relevant professional body. More than 5 years’ experience in teaching at Masters level or higher or in medical, health or professional practice-based education settings. Research experience with a demonstrated track record. Strong communication and stakeholder management skills. Ability to work independently and in multidisciplinary teams. Commitment to student support and pastoral care. Knowledge of research methods, ethics, and governance processes. Strong project management skills, including managing multiple priorities and milestones. Supports the mission and values of Notre Dame as a Catholic University. A ctive research involvement, and experience in university/research settings or student research supervision highly regarded. Benefits Fitness Passport on offer. Generous training and development opportunities. Friendly and collaborative working environment. How to apply Please apply online with a resume and cover letter outlining your suitability for the role. Applications closing date: 22 October 2026 For further information on how to apply, please visit: How to apply | Notre Dame Applicants are expected to have current and valid work rights in Australia. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people are encouraged to apply. The University is committed to providing a safe, respectful and inclusive working and learning environment. The successful candidate will be required to complete a self-declaration regarding Gender-Based Violence (as required by the National Code) as part of the pre-employment process. For more information on this please refer to our website. The University of Notre Dame Australia seeks to increase the diversity of our workforce to better meet the different needs of the University and its stakeholders and to improve equal opportunity outcomes for our staff. Contact us Please direct your queries regarding this role to Melissa Schmidt , Melissa.schmidt@nd.edu.au Unsolicited contact from recruitment agencies will not be entertained. Back to current openings"
    },
    "https://www.notredame.edu.au/about-us/jobs-at-unda/employment-opportunities/job-details/_nocache?jobid=oPORAfwO": {
      "title": "Senior Lecturer/Associate Professor (Pathological Sciences), School of Medicine",
      "description": "Connect on social media Connect with Notre Dame on Social Media Australia LinkedIn X YouTube TikTok Fremantle Facebook Instagram Broome Facebook Instagram Sydney Facebook Instagram Senior Lecturer/Associate Professor (Pathological Sciences), School of Medicine APPLY How to Apply Position Description Need help? Ask Notre Dame For further information or enquiries, please contact People and Culture: jobs@nd.edu.au Employment Type Part-Time Category Medicine Location Sydney Department School of Medicine Share LinkedIn Facebook X Email Senior Lecturer/Associate Professor (Pathological Sciences), School of Medicine Senior Lecturer/Associate Professor (Pathological Sciences), School of Medicine Sydney Campus Part-time opportunity (0.5), Continuing contract Friendly and collaborative work environment Rewarding opportunity offering career development Level C from $147,864 - D $176,899 + 15.00% superannuation About the university We believe in the power of education to transform lives, strengthen communities and help people become the best version of themselves. The University of Notre Dame Australia is a modern, national Catholic university that serves more than 12,000 students across its campuses in Fremantle, Broome and Sydney. With a community of over 1,000 academic and professional staff, Notre Dame is recognised for its personalised learning environment, strong graduate outcomes and holistic educational philosophy rooted in critical thinking, ethics and the Catholic intellectual tradition. About the role You will lead the coordination and development of Year 1 and 2 Pathology teaching, working collaboratively with colleagues across Sydney, Fremantle, and Broome to deliver an integrated biological sciences curriculum within the MD program. Key Responsibilities Coordinate and deliver Pathology teaching across the MD Foundation Years. Collaborate with curriculum, academic, and clinical teams to integrate Pathology learning across the MD program. Maintain and enhance curriculum resources, ensuring they are current, evidence-based, and innovative. Support the recruitment, training, and development of Pathology tutors and sessional educators. Partner with assessment and curriculum teams to align learning outcomes and assessments. Design, deliver, and mark formative and summative assessments. Deliver high-quality, engaging Pathology and BCS teaching. Drive continuous improvement through educational scholarship and evidence-based practice. Use student feedback and engagement data to enhance teaching and learning outcomes. Contribute to domain meetings, committees, and School activities. Represent the BCS Domain and support broader School and University initiatives. Qualifications, skills & experience Level C Senior Lecturer Possession of a Medical qualification registrable with AHPRA and a Fellowship of a relevant professional college. Expertise in the Pathological Sciences and preferably Experience in teaching at Masters level or higher or in medical, health sciences or professional practice-based education settings. Experience delivering engaging teaching across face-to-face, online, and blended environments. Strong team collaboration and leadership skills. Experience in collaborative and student-centred learning. Commitment to student support and pastoral care. Interest in educational scholarship and professional development. Experience using educational technologies and learning platforms. Excellent communication and interpersonal skills. Level D Associate Professor Possession of a Medical qualification registrable with AHPRA and a Fellowship of a relevant professional college. Advanced expertise and recognised standing in the Pathological Sciences, with substantial experience in teaching at Masters level or higher and/or in medical, health sciences or professional practice-based education settings. Sustained academic achievement at Associate Professor level. Extensive experience delivering high-quality teaching across multiple modes. Proven academic leadership and team management capability. Strong commitment to student engagement, wellbeing, and pastoral care. Ongoing contribution to scholarship, research, and professional practice. Skilled in educational technologies and innovative teaching approaches. Excellent communication, collaboration, and stakeholder engagement skills. Ability to influence and contribute to strategic and disciplinary priorities. Benefits Fitness Passport on offer. Generous training and development opportunities. Friendly and collaborative working environment. How to apply Please apply online with a resume and cover letter outlining your suitability for the role. Applications closing date: 23 October 2026 For further information on how to apply, please visit: How to apply | Notre Dame Applicants are expected to have current and valid work rights in Australia. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people are encouraged to apply. The University is committed to providing a safe, respectful and inclusive working and learning environment. The successful candidate will be required to complete a self-declaration regarding Gender-Based Violence (as required by the National Code) as part of the pre-employment process. For more information on this please refer to our website. The University of Notre Dame Australia seeks to increase the diversity of our workforce to better meet the different needs of the University and its stakeholders and to improve equal opportunity outcomes for our staff. Contact us Please direct your queries regarding this role to Melissa Schmidt , Melissa.schmidt@nd.edu.au Unsolicited contact from recruitment agencies will not be entertained. Back to current openings"
    },
    "https://www.notredame.edu.au/about-us/jobs-at-unda/employment-opportunities/job-details/_nocache?jobid=ouKzAfw7": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow in Hebrew Philosophy",
      "description": "Connect on social media Connect with Notre Dame on Social Media Australia LinkedIn X YouTube TikTok Fremantle Facebook Instagram Broome Facebook Instagram Sydney Facebook Instagram Postdoctoral Research Fellow in Hebrew Philosophy APPLY How to Apply Position Description Need help? Ask Notre Dame For further information or enquiries, please contact People and Culture: jobs@nd.edu.au Employment Type Full-Time Category Research Location Sydney Department PVC Research Share LinkedIn Facebook X Email Postdoctoral Research Fellow in Hebrew Philosophy Postdoctoral Research Fellow ( Hebrew Philosophy ) Sydney Campus 2 x F ull-time opportunit ies , Fixed term contract for 3 years Friendly and collaborative work environment Rewarding opportunity offering career developmen t $121,739 pa (Level B) + 1 5 % super annuation About the university We believe in the power of education to transform lives, strengthen communities and help people become the best version of themselves. The University of Notre Dame Australia is a modern, national Catholic university that serves more than 12,000 students across its campuses in Fremantle, Broome and Sydney. With a community of over 1,000 academic and professional staff, Notre Dame is recognised for its personalised learning environment, strong graduate outcomes and holistic educational philosophy rooted in critical thinking, ethics and the Catholic intellectual tradition. About the project The University of Notre Dame Australia is proud to host the Arabic and Hebrew teams of the E uropean R esearch C ouncil (ERC) Synergy project, “Logic in Reverse Redux: Illegitimate Argumentative Moves in the Arabic, Byzantine, Hebrew and Latin Medieval Traditions,” ( RevLogRedux ), which is to run until 30 April 2031. The coordinating team of the Synergy Grant is led by Leone Gazziero at the CNRS , France ; the Arabic team leader at UNDA is Tony Street and the Hebrew team leader is Charles Manekin. We seek to appoint up to two highly research-active scholars working in medieval Hebrew philosophy with a track-record, or an interest in developing expertise, in researching medieval logical texts and fallacy-theory. In accordance with ERC conditions, all outcomes are to be published Open Access. About the role The Hebrew Tradition team of the ERC project, “Logic in Reverse Redux: Illegitimate Argumentative Moves in the Arabic, Byzantine, Hebrew and Latin Medieval Traditions,” seeks to appoint up to two postdoctoral researchers. Participation in the project will be an opportunity to acquire new textual and philosophical skills, produce a significant research portfolio, develop ties with leading researchers in medieval philosophy, and be part of a major initiative in digital humanities. The team will engage actively with the other teams working on the Latin, Byzantine, Arabic rational and Arabic traditional sciences. As a guide , appointees will be expected to devote their time as follows: 20% transcribing , editing and translating a number of texts; 20% responding to the initiatives of the Geneva team as they develop AskSten ; 60% conducting independent research on the historical and cultural context of the texts studied, and analysis of the fallacy-theories developed by Hebrew authors, especially as they relate to Aristotle’s Sophistical Refutations . Th ese research-focused position s will contribute to the University's strategic research goals through the production of high-impact publications, including ERC-related outputs targeting leading Q1 journals or scholarly monograph s . The role will strengthen the research profile of the Centre for the History of Philosophy, particularly in the growing field of Hebrew Philosophy, while supporting grant applications, research supervision and mentoring, and engag ing in academic, professional, and research administration activities. Key Responsibilities Contribute to the production of critical editions and translations of a corpus of medieval Hebrew texts on fallacies (see here for passages to be edited), preferably as the sole editor and translator for assigned passages, or, where necessary, as a team member working under the supervision of Professor Manekin. Undertake the transcription of texts not yet available in electronic format for inclusion in the project's web-based database. Conduct primary research on the historical and cultural context of the texts studied, and analysis of the fallacy-theories developed by Hebrew authors, especially as they relate to Aristotle’s Sophistical Refutations . Collaborate with the University of Geneva team in integrating materials from four philosophical traditions into AskSten , a multi-layered virtual research environment whose digital archive, knowledge base, Semantic Web architecture, and online deployment will enhance the formal expressiveness and coherence of the project's findings; see https://www.unige.ch/RevLog/about-1 . Contribute to all activities associated with the ERC Synergy Grant, including participating in conferences and workshops in the UK and Europe, help in organising conferences and workshop s , translation, independent research on topics related to the texts to be edited , and liaison with teams at CNRS, the University of Lille, and the University of Geneva to advance the project's objectives of collaboration and synergy. Post-docs are expected to participate in person in a fortnightly team seminar through the semester at the Broadway Campus. Qualifications, skills & experience Essential: Research-level proficiency in the philosophical language of medieval Hebrew . Doctoral level training in Hebrew philosophy or medieval Jewish intellectual history, with evidence of potential for high-quality research ; a strong publication record is highly desirable. Proficiency in Hebrew text entry, with the ability to read and interpret medieval manuscripts. Desirable : B ackground in digital humanities . Facility in another language in RevLog’s coverage (Latin, classical Greek, Arabic ) or relevant to the study of the broader translation movement (Syriac). How to apply Please apply online with a resume and cover letter outlining your suitability for the role. Applications closing date: 6 November 2026 Applications will be assessed progressively as they are received. These opportunities are available to candidates with valid Australian working rights and to international applicants seeking employment in Australia. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people are encouraged to apply. The University is committed to providing a safe, respectful and inclusive working and learning environment. The successful candidate will be required to complete a self-declaration regarding Gender-Based Violence (as required by the National Code) as part of the pre-employment process. For more information on this please refer to our website. The University of Notre Dame Australia seeks to increase the diversity of our workforce to meet the different needs of the University and its stakeholders better and to improve equal opportunity outcomes for our staff. Contact us Please direct your queries about academic aspects of the project to Hebrew team leader Charles Manekin manekinc@umd.edu Arabic team leader Tony Street tony.street@nd.edu.au Talent Acquisition Partner Haritha Garlapti, haritha.garlapati@nd.edu.au Back to current openings"
    },
    "https://www.notredame.edu.au/about-us/jobs-at-unda/employment-opportunities/job-details/_nocache?jobid=opKzAfw2": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow in Arabic Philosophy",
      "description": "Connect on social media Connect with Notre Dame on Social Media Australia LinkedIn X YouTube TikTok Fremantle Facebook Instagram Broome Facebook Instagram Sydney Facebook Instagram Postdoctoral Research Fellow in Arabic Philosophy APPLY How to Apply Position Description Need help? Ask Notre Dame For further information or enquiries, please contact People and Culture: jobs@nd.edu.au Employment Type Full-Time Category Research Location Sydney Department PVC Research Share LinkedIn Facebook X Email Postdoctoral Research Fellow in Arabic Philosophy Postdoctoral Research Fellow in Arabic Philosophy Sydney Campus 3 x F ull-time opportunit ies , Fixed term contract for 3 years Friendly and collaborative work environment Rewarding opportunity offering career developmen t Level B $121,739 AUD + 1 5 . 0 0% super annuation per annum About the university We believe in the power of education to transform lives, strengthen communities and help people become the best version of themselves. The University of Notre Dame Australia is a modern, national Catholic university that serves more than 12,000 students across its campuses in Fremantle, Broome and Sydney. With a community of over 1,000 academic and professional staff, Notre Dame is recognised for its personalised learning environment, strong graduate outcomes and holistic educational philosophy rooted in critical thinking, ethics and the Catholic intellectual tradition. About the project The University of Notre Dame Australia is proud to host the Arabic and Hebrew teams of the E uropean R esearch C ouncil (ERC) Synergy project, “Logic in Reverse Redux: Illegitimate Argumentative Moves in the Arabic, Byzantine, Hebrew and Latin Medieval Traditions,” (RevLogRedux), which is to run until 30 April 2031. The coordinating team of the Synergy Grant is led by Leone Gazziero at the CNRS , France ; the Arabic team leader at UNDA is Tony Street ; and the Hebrew team leader is Charles Manekin. We seek to appoint up to three highly research-active scholars working in medieval Arabic philosophy with a track-record, or an interest in developing expertise, in researching medieval logical texts and fallacy-theory. In accordance with ERC conditions, all outcomes are to be published Open Access. About the role The Arabic Tradition: Rational Sciences (hereafter, ATRS) team of the ERC project, “Logic in Reverse Redux: Illegitimate Argumentative Moves in the Arabic, Byzantine, Hebrew and Latin Medieval Traditions,” seeks to appoint up to three postdoctoral researchers. Participation in the project will be an opportunity to acquire new textual and philosophical skills, produce a significant research portfolio, develop ties with leading researchers in medieval philosophy, and be part of a major initiative in digital humanities. The ATRS team will engage actively with the other teams working on the Latin, Byzantine, Hebrew and Arabic traditional sciences. As a guide, appointees will be expected to devote their time as follows: 20% transcribing and translating a number of texts, either individually or under guidance from the team leader; 20% responding to the initiatives of the Geneva team as they develop AskSten; 60% conducting independent research on the historical and cultural context of the texts studied, and analysis of the fallacy-theories developed by Arabic authors, especially as they relate to Aristotle’s Sophistical Refutations . Th ese research-focused position s will contribute to the University's strategic research goals through the production of high-impact publications, including ERC-related outputs targeting leading Q1 journals or scholarly monograph s . The role will strengthen the research profile of the Centre for the History of Philosophy, particularly in the growing field of Arabic Philosophy, while supporting grant applications, research supervision and mentoring, and engag ing in academic, professional, and research administration activities. Key Responsibilities Contribute to the translation of a corpus of medieval Arabic texts on fallacies, preferably as the sole translator for assigned passages (see here for passages already earmarked for translation), or, where necessary, as a team member working under the supervision of a primary translator. Each appointee will be expected to translate approximately 150 pages over the three-year project period. Undertake the transcription of texts not yet available in electronic format for inclusion in the project's web-based database. Conduct primary research on the historical and cultural context of the texts studied, and analysis of the fallacy-theories developed by Arabic authors, especially as they relate to Aristotle’s Sophistical Refutations . Collaborate with the University of Geneva team in integrating materials from four philosophical traditions into AskSten, a multi-layered virtual research environment whose digital archive, knowledge base, Semantic Web architecture, and online deployment will enhance the formal expressiveness and coherence of the project's findings; see https://www.unige.ch/RevLog/about-1 . Contribute to all activities associated with the ERC Synergy Grant, including participating in conferences and workshops in the UK and Europe, help in organising conferences and workshop s , translation, independent research on topics related to the texts listed at WEBSITE , and liaison with teams at CNRS, the University of Lille, and the University of Geneva to advance the project's objectives of collaboration and synergy. Post-docs are expected to participate in person in a fortnightly team seminar through the semester at the Broadway Campus. Qualifications, skills & experience Essential: Research-level proficiency in the philosophical language of medieval Arabic. Doctoral level training in Arabic philosophy or medieval Islamic intellectual history, with evidence of potential for high-quality research ; a strong publication record is highly desirable. Proficiency in Arabic text entry, with the ability to read and interpret medieval manuscripts. Desirable : B ackground in digital humanities . Facility in another language in RevLog’s coverage (Latin, classical Greek, Hebrew) or relevant to the study of the Arabic translation movement (Syriac). How to apply Please apply online with a resume and cover letter outlining your suitability for the role. Applications closing date: 6 November 2026 Applications will be assessed progressively as they are received. These opportunities are available to candidates with valid Australian working rights and to international applicants seeking employment in Australia . Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people are encouraged to apply. The University is committed to providing a safe, respectful and inclusive working and learning environment. The successful candidate will be required to complete a self-declaration regarding Gender-Based Violence (as required by the National Code) as part of the pre-employment process. For more information on this please refer to our website. The University of Notre Dame Australia seeks to increase the diversity of our workforce to meet the different needs of the University and its stakeholders better and to improve equal opportunity outcomes for our staff. Contact us Please direct your queries about academic aspects of the project to : Arabic Team Leader , Professor Tony Street, Tony.street@nd.edu.au Talent Acquisition Melissa Schmidt, Melissa.schmidt@nd.edu.au Back to current openings"
    },
    "https://www.notredame.edu.au/about-us/jobs-at-unda/employment-opportunities/job-details/_nocache?jobid=oPtyxfw7": {
      "title": "Senior Chaplaincy Officer",
      "description": "Connect on social media Connect with Notre Dame on Social Media Australia LinkedIn X YouTube TikTok Fremantle Facebook Instagram Broome Facebook Instagram Sydney Facebook Instagram Senior Chaplaincy Officer APPLY How to Apply Position Description Need help? Ask Notre Dame For further information or enquiries, please contact People and Culture: jobs@nd.edu.au Employment Type Full-Time Category Other Location Sydney Department Chaplaincy Share LinkedIn Facebook X Email Senior Chaplaincy Officer Senior Chaplaincy Officer, Chaplaincy Offic e Sydney campus Full-time opportunity, Continuing c ontract Help bring the Catholic intellectual tradition to life as part of campus life. $ 116,695 pa (level) 7 + 15% superannuation About the university We believe in the power of education to transform lives, strengthen communities and help people become the best version of themselves. The University of Notre Dame Australia is a modern, national Catholic university that serves more than 12,000 students across its campuses in Fremantle, Broome and Sydney. With a community of over 1,000 academic and professional staff, Notre Dame is recognised for its personalised learning environment, strong graduate outcomes and holistic educational philosophy rooted in critical thinking, ethics and the Catholic intellectual tradition . About the office The Chaplaincy team supports the spiritual and intellectual life of the campus by creating a welcoming, inclusive environment where students and staff can explore faith, engage with Catholic social teaching and contribute to service and social justice initiatives. About the role The Senior Chaplaincy Officer leads and inspires campus Chaplaincy, bringing the Catholic intellectual tradition to life through faith, service and community. This role helps strengthen Notre Dame’s mission and nurture a vibrant spiritual life across campus. Key Responsibilities Be present on campus and provide pastoral care and support to students and staff. Support major University events, including Orientation, Graduation Masses and Open Days. Help run the Chaplaincy Office, including administration, budgets, events and records. Create faith and spiritual programs that support students and staff. Plan spiritual activities such as retreats, prayer, scripture readings and Mass. Welcome people of all faith backgrounds and help create an inclusive community. Build strong partnerships with local parishes, dioceses and Catholic organisations. Qualifications, skills & experience A relevant qualification and experience in Chaplaincy, Religious Education, mission, campus ministry or a related area. A strong understanding of Catholic teachings, traditions and the role of Catholic education. Experience leading or supporting faith formation, pastoral care, events or community activities. Strong communication skills and the ability to build warm, respectful relationships. Good organisational skills, including the ability to manage priorities, budgets and resources. Confident using Microsoft Office and online communication tools. Support for Notre Dame’s mission and values as a Catholic University Vision and objects | Notre Dame . Benefits Grow your skills with training and development opportunities. Join a friendly, supportive and collaborative team. Access discounted study at Notre Dame. How to apply Please apply online with a resume and cover letter outlining your suitability for the role. Applications closing date: 26 October 2026 For further information on how to apply, please visit: How to apply | Notre Dame Applicants are expected to have current and valid work rights in Australia. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people are encouraged to apply. The University is committed to providing a safe, respectful and inclusive working and learning environment. The successful candidate will be required to complete a self-declaration regarding Gender-Based Violence (as required by the National Code) as part of the pre-employment process. For more information on this please refer to our website. The University of Notre Dame Australia seeks to increase the diversity of our workforce to better meet the different needs of the University and its stakeholders and to improve equal opportunity outcomes for our staff. Contact us Please direct your queries regarding this role to Marcia Rodrigues , Marcia.Rodrigues@nd.edu.au Unsolicited contact from recruitment agencies will not be entertained. Back to current openings"
