    "https://www.notredame.edu.au/about-us/jobs-at-unda/employment-opportunities/job-details/_nocache?jobid=octNAfwM": {
      "title": "Laboratory Officer, School of Health Sciences",
      "description": "Connect on social media Connect with Notre Dame on Social Media Australia LinkedIn X YouTube TikTok Fremantle Facebook Instagram Broome Facebook Instagram Sydney Facebook Instagram Laboratory Officer, School of Health Sciences APPLY How to Apply Position Description Need help? Ask Notre Dame For further information or enquiries, please contact People and Culture: jobs@nd.edu.au Employment Type Full-Time Category Education & Training Location Fremantle Department School of Health Sciences Share LinkedIn Facebook X Email Laboratory Officer, School of Health Sciences Laboratory Officer , School of Health Sciences Fremantle Campus Full-time opportunity, Continuing c ontract Friendly and collaborative environment Rewarding opportunity $ 82,752 pa (level 5 ) + 1 5 % superannuation About the university We believe in the power of education to transform lives, strengthen communities and help people become the best version of themselves. The University of Notre Dame Australia is a modern, national Catholic university that serves more than 12,000 students across its campuses in Fremantle, Broome and Sydney. With a community of over 1,000 academic and professional staff, Notre Dame is recognised for its personalised learning environment, strong graduate outcomes and holistic educational philosophy rooted in critical thinking, ethics and the Catholic intellectual tradition . About the school The School of Health Sciences offers an extensive range of quality accredited undergraduate and postgraduate programs designed to engage students from learning opportunities in class through to professional workplace experience. About the role The Laboratory Officer is responsible for the efficient provision and operation of laboratory services, including the preparation and maintenance of equipment, resources, and facilities. Working closely with academic and professional staff, the role supports teaching, research, and practical learning while ensuring safety, organisation, and compliance with relevant policies . In this role, you will be responsible for setup and pack down of s imulation l aboratory spaces, and relevant equipment. Primarily supporting the Exercise and Sport Science, Clinical Exercise Physiology, and Physiotherapy courses. Key Responsibilities Provide technical support to academic staff, researchers, HDR students, and students during practical classes, experiments, and research activities. Prepare, maintain, calibrate, and troubleshoot laboratory equipment to ensure it is safe, functional, and ready for use. Manage laboratory inventory, resources, documentation, and records, including maintenance schedules, equipment condition reports, and preparation notes. Maintain laboratory safety and compliance through risk assessments, safety audits, standard operating procedures, and implementation of safety practices. Coordinate with internal and external stakeholders for equipment servicing, and procurement of laboratory materials . Support the continuous improvement of laboratory operations, collaborating with academic and professional staff to enhance teaching, research, and student learning outcomes. Qualifications, skills & experience Relevant tertiary degree or a qualification such as an advanced diploma with at least 1 year of relevant experience, a diploma with 2 years of experience, a Certificate IV with extensive experience, a post-trades certificate with more than 2 years of experience as a technician, or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Proven laboratory experience, preferably within a tertiary or higher education environment, including the operation and demonstration of laboratory equipment in learning and teaching settings. Strong knowledge of laboratory operations, equipment maintenance, inventory management, servicing, and laboratory safety protocols. Excellent organisational, administrative, and operational skills, with the ability to manage multiple competing priorities and maintain accurate records and documentation. Effective verbal and written communication skills, with the ability to liaise with staff, students, suppliers, and prepare high-quality reports and documentation. Demonstrated problem-solving ability, strong attention to detail, proficiency in Microsoft Office and related systems, and the ability to collaborate across teams while contributing to continuous improvement initiatives. Benefits Fast paced environment with opportunity for professional growth Generous training and development opportunities Friendly and collaborative working environment How to apply Please apply online with a resume and cover letter outlining your suitability for the role. Applications closing date: 9 November 2026 For further information on how to apply, please visit: How to apply | Notre Dame Applicants are expected to have current and valid work rights in Australia. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people are encouraged to apply. The University is committed to providing a safe, respectful and inclusive working and learning environment. The successful candidate will be required to complete a self-declaration regarding Gender-Based Violence (as required by the National Code) as part of the pre-employment process. For more information on this please refer to our website. The University of Notre Dame Australia seeks to increase the diversity of our workforce to better meet the different needs of the University and its stakeholders and to improve equal opportunity outcomes for our staff. Contact us Please direct your queries regarding this role to Haritha Garlapati , Haritha.Garlapati@nd.edu.au Unsolicited contact from recruitment agencies will not be entertained. Back to current openings"
    },
    "https://www.notredame.edu.au/about-us/jobs-at-unda/employment-opportunities/job-details/_nocache?jobid=opVPAfwt": {
      "title": "Lecturer (Physiotherapy), School of Health Sciences",
      "description": "Connect on social media Connect with Notre Dame on Social Media Australia LinkedIn X YouTube TikTok Fremantle Facebook Instagram Broome Facebook Instagram Sydney Facebook Instagram Lecturer (Physiotherapy), School of Health Sciences APPLY How to Apply Position Description Need help? Ask Notre Dame For further information or enquiries, please contact People and Culture: jobs@nd.edu.au Employment Type Full-Time Category Education & Training Location Sydney Department School of Health Sciences Share LinkedIn Facebook X Email Lecturer (Physiotherapy), School of Health Sciences Lecturer (Physiotherapy) , School of Health Sciences Sydney (Broadway) Campus Full-time opportunity, Continuing c ontract (FTE negotiable for a successful candidate) Fast paced environment with opportunity for professional growth Friendly and collaborative working environment From $121,739 pa (level B ) + 1 5 % superannuation About the university We believe in the power of education to transform lives, strengthen communities and help people become the best version of themselves. The University of Notre Dame Australia is a modern, national Catholic university that serves more than 12,000 students across its campuses in Fremantle, Broome and Sydney. With a community of over 1,000 academic and professional staff, Notre Dame is recognised for its personalised learning environment, strong graduate outcomes and holistic educational philosophy rooted in critical thinking, ethics and the Catholic intellectual tradition . About the school The School of Health Sciences offers an extensive range of quality accredited undergraduate and postgraduate programs designed to engage students from learning opportunities in class through to professional workplace experience . About the role The School of Health Sciences is seeking to appoint a passionate and experienced physiotherapist to teach into the Master of Physiotherapy program. This role also offers the opportunity to contribute to teaching in other foundational areas of Health Sciences aligned with your academic and professional expertise . We are specifically seeking expertise in cardiorespiratory, paediatric and/or neurological physiotherapy. 1 FTE is available, however fractional appointments will be considered for suitable candidates. Key Responsibilities Lead delivery of physiotherapy curriculum in the areas of cardiorespiratory, paediatric and/or neurological physiotherapy. Course coordination of allocated courses, including planning, scheduling, delivery of teaching activities and assessments. Prepare, update, and maintain course materials to ensure alignment with current clinical practice and academic standards. Assess student learning through marking, feedback, and supporting academic progress and development. Participate in research, quality improvement, and engagement activities with internal and external stakeholders. Undertake administrative duties and comply with university policies, procedures. Provide pastoral care and consultation to support student wellbeing throughout their university experience. Qualifications, skills & experience Possession of, or progress toward, a doctoral qualification in Physiotherapy, or equivalent clinical and/or academic experience with relevant postgraduate qualifications Current full registration as a physiotherapist with the Physiotherapists Registration Board under AHPRA. Experience in delivering educational materials in a variety of formats, which may include entry-level, postgraduate or professional-level education. Demonstrated excellent administrative and organisational skills. Strong oral and written communication skills, with the ability to work collaboratively within a multidisciplinary team environment. A strong commitment to continuous quality improvement and support for the Objects of the University. Capacity to contribute to curriculum development, pedagogical innovation and research initiatives (desirable). Benefits Growing and highly successful School, with opportunity for professional and personal growth Friendly and collaborative working environment Flexible working environment How to apply Please apply online with a resume and a brief cover letter outlining your suitability for the role. Applications closing date: 3 November 2026 For further information on how to apply, please visit: How to apply | Notre Dame Applicants are expected to have current and valid work rights in Australia. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people are encouraged to apply. The University is committed to providing a safe, respectful and inclusive working and learning environment. The successful candidate will be required to complete a self-declaration regarding Gender-Based Violence (as required by the National Code) as part of the pre-employment process. For more information on this please refer to our website. The University of Notre Dame Australia seeks to increase the diversity of our workforce to better meet the different needs of the University and its stakeholders and to improve equal opportunity outcomes for our staff. Contact us Please direct your queries regarding this role to Robyn Sturdy, National Physiotherapy Program Coordinator robyn.sturdy1@nd.edu.au Unsolicited contact from recruitment agencies will not be entertained. Back to current openings"
    },
    "https://www.notredame.edu.au/about-us/jobs-at-unda/employment-opportunities/job-details/_nocache?jobid=oTmXAfww": {
      "title": "Senior Lecturer / Associate Professor (Program Coordinator)",
      "description": "Connect on social media Connect with Notre Dame on Social Media Australia LinkedIn X YouTube TikTok Fremantle Facebook Instagram Broome Facebook Instagram Sydney Facebook Instagram Senior Lecturer / Associate Professor (Program Coordinator) APPLY How to Apply Position Description Need help? Ask Notre Dame For further information or enquiries, please contact People and Culture: jobs@nd.edu.au Employment Type Full-Time Category Education & Training Location Fremantle Department School of Health Sciences Share LinkedIn Facebook X Email Senior Lecturer / Associate Professor (Program Coordinator) Senior Lecturer / Associate Professor (Program Coordinator) , School of Health Sciences Fremantle Campus Full-time opportunity, Continuing c ontract (FTE negotiable for a successful candidate) Friendly and collaborative environment Rewarding opportunity $ 142,456 pa (level C ) + 1 5 % superannuation $171,534 pa (level D) + 15% superannuation About the university We believe in the power of education to transform lives, strengthen communities and help people become the best version of themselves. The University of Notre Dame Australia is a modern, national Catholic university that serves more than 12,000 students across its campuses in Fremantle, Broome and Sydney. With a community of over 1,000 academic and professional staff, Notre Dame is recognised for its personalised learning environment, strong graduate outcomes and holistic educational philosophy rooted in critical thinking, ethics and the Catholic intellectual tradition . About the school The School of Health Sciences is an innovative and supportive environment, focused on educating the health practitioners and leaders of tomorrow. The School of Health Sciences offers an extensive range of quality accredited undergraduate and postgraduate programs designed to engage students from learning opportunities in class through to professional workplace experience. About the role Based at the Fremantle campus, the Program Coordinator (Speech Pathology) provides academic leadership and oversight of the Speech Pathology program at the Fremantle Campus, ensuring high-quality, evidence-informed delivery aligned with accreditation requirements and University priorities. The Master of Speech Pathology (Qualifying) admitted its first cohort in Sydney in 2026 and is undergoing accreditation with Speech Pathology Australia. The inaugural cohort of speech pathology students at the Fremantle campus will start in July 2027. The program coordinator will work collaboratively with the discipline lead for speech pathology to deliver identical content to students in Fremantle and to prepare the Fremantle accreditation submission ahead of a Speech Pathology Australia site visit in early 2028. The successful applicant will work with academic and professional colleagues to support curriculum implementation in Fremantle, support additional staff members and ensure program governance, student progression and strong graduate outcomes. Key Responsibilities Curriculum, teaching and quality Deliver and support other staff to deliver high-quality Speech Pathology curriculum in lectures, tutorials, demonstrations in person and online. Contribute to the curriculum development in their clinical areas of expertise across both campuses to ensure teaching and assessment align with accreditation standards, contemporary practice and University requirements. Support, mark and moderate authentic assessments and ensure provision of high-quality student feedback taking into consideration the learning outcomes, and academic integrity. Use student feedback, staff reflections and peer feedback to guide continuous improvement and to maintain academic integrity Ensure research enhanced teaching across all domains of speech pathology Ensure teaching and assessments continue to align with accreditation approval criteria or justify and document any changes Student Experience and Outcomes Promote a positive and student-centred learning environment. Meet with individual and groups of students to support their learning Contribute to student recruitment, orientation, progression and retention strategies . Research, Scholarship and Engagement Maintain an appropriate profile in research, scholarship and/or professional practice consistent with the academic level. Promote research-informed teaching and contribute to collaborative scholarly activity and student research supervision, where appropriate. Develop productive relationships with industry partners, professional bodies and community stakeholders. Represent and promote the Speech Pathology program in relevant internal and external forums. Leadership Lead, mentor and support academic and sessional staff, fostering a collaborative, high-performing and scholarly academic culture. Work with the discipline lead to ensure successful accreditation and program assurance, including oversight of relevant evidence, risks, improvement actions and clinical education requirements. Build strong relationships with health services, industry, professional bodies and community partners, while contributing to research, scholarship and broader School initiatives. Qualifications, skills & experience In addition to the requirements of the Academic Classification Level C or D that underpin this role, the following apply : Senior Lecturer (Level C) Doctoral qualification in Speech Pathology, Speech and Language Therapy or a closely related field, or equivalent accreditation and professional standing. Current membership of, or eligibility for, Certified Practising Speech Pathologist status with Speech Pathology Australia (SPA). Substantial higher education experience in speech pathology, including teaching, curriculum development, assessment and course coordination. Experience contributing to accreditation, quality assurance and academic governance, with strong knowledge of contemporary speech pathology practice and professional standards. A record of scholarly and professional achievement, such as independent research, peer-reviewed publication, conference presentations and research student supervision. Ability to lead and support teaching teams and to build effective relationships with clinical education partners and stakeholders. Commitment to inclusive education, cultural responsiveness, academic integrity and student success. Supportive of the Objects of Notre Dame as a Catholic University. Associate Professor (Level D) Doctoral qualification in Speech Pathology, Speech and Language Therapy or a closely related field, or equivalent accreditation and professional standing. Current membership of, or eligibility for, Certified Practising Speech Pathologist status with Speech Pathology Australia (SPA). Extensive higher education experience in speech pathology, including leadership of curriculum, assessment and delivery at program level. Demonstrated leadership in accreditation, quality assurance and academic governance, such as leading a program through professional accreditation or reaccreditation. National recognition in speech pathology education, research or professional practice, with a sustained record of publication, research funding and supervision of research students to completion. Proven ability to lead, mentor and develop academic colleagues and to build partnerships with health services, professional bodies and community organisations. Commitment to inclusive education, cultural responsiveness, academic integrity and student success. Supportive of the Objects of Notre Dame as a Catholic University. Benefits Fast paced environment with opportunity for professional growth Generous training and development opportunities Friendly and collaborative working environment How to apply Please apply online with a resume and cover letter outlining your suitability for the role. Applications closing date: 10 November 2026 For further information on how to apply, please visit: How to apply | Notre Dame Applicants are expected to have current and valid work rights in Australia. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people are encouraged to apply. The University is committed to providing a safe, respectful and inclusive working and learning environment. The successful candidate will be required to complete a self-declaration regarding Gender-Based Violence (as required by the National Code) as part of the pre-employment process. For more information on this please refer to our website. The University of Notre Dame Australia seeks to increase the diversity of our workforce to better meet the different needs of the University and its stakeholders and to improve equal opportunity outcomes for our staff. Contact us Please direct your queries regarding this role to Associate Professor Andy Smidt, andy.smidt@nd.edu.au Unsolicited contact from recruitment agencies will not be entertained. Back to current openings"
    },
    "https://www.notredame.edu.au/about-us/jobs-at-unda/employment-opportunities/job-details/_nocache?jobid=oMWSzfwT": {
      "title": "Trainer and Assessor (Nursing), VET",
      "description": "Connect on social media Connect with Notre Dame on Social Media Australia LinkedIn X YouTube TikTok Fremantle Facebook Instagram Broome Facebook Instagram Sydney Facebook Instagram Trainer and Assessor (Nursing), VET APPLY How to Apply Position Description Need help? Ask Notre Dame For further information or enquiries, please contact People and Culture: jobs@nd.edu.au Employment Type Full-Time Category Education & Training Location Fremantle Department Vocational Education & Training Share LinkedIn Facebook X Email Trainer and Assessor (Nursing), VET Trainer and Assessor , (Nursing) V ET Fremantle campus Full-time opportunity, Continuing c ontract Join the University supporting its students and staff to achieve their career goals Be part of a welcoming community of professionals, academics and students Competitive Salary + 15% superannuation About the university We believe in the power of education to transform lives, strengthen communities and help people become the best version of themselves. The University of Notre Dame Australia is a modern, national Catholic university that serves more than 12,000 students across its campuses in Fremantle, Broome and Sydney. With a community of over 1,000 academic and professional staff, Notre Dame is recognised for its personalised learning environment, strong graduate outcomes and holistic educational philosophy rooted in critical thinking, ethics and the Catholic intellectual tradition. About the office Vocational Education and Training (VET) at Notre Dame provides students with the skills they need to thrive in the education and health industries. We provide career preparation and formal qualifications to specialise in a certain area of work, to make a career change or as a pathway into higher education. Our programs are flexible and structured so students can learn online, on-campus and in the field. About the role The Trainer and Assessor will be responsible to plan, develop and deliver vocational education training and assessment in accordance with the relevant training packages, Standards for Registered Training Organisations (RTOs) 20 25 and the Australian Qualifications Framework. The Trainer and Assessor plays a crucial role in equipping our students with practical skills and knowledge that align with current industry requirements, thereby enhancing their employability and career progression. Key Responsibilities Training Delivery – Conduct training sessions, develop training materials, assessment methods adapt training methods and deliver. Assessment and Evaluation – Assess learner competency, provide feedback, maintain records and participate in validation work processes. Learner Support – Support learners, monitor student progress and encourage development for a positive learning experience. Quality Assurance – Adhere to training standards, ensure continuous improvement, and compliance standards are achieved. Qualifications, skills & experience Essential requirements Hold a Bachelor of Nursing Qualification (Category A-C). Current registration as a Registered Nurse with the Nursing and Midwifery Board of Australia (NMBA) via the Australian Health Practitioner Regulation Agency (AHPRA). Hold a Certificate IV in Training and Assessment - TAE40166 or TAE40122. Skills and Experience Strong industry knowledge and vocational competency relevant to the training area of nursing, including current standards and best practice. Ongoing professional development in vocational education and competency-based training and assessment. Effective interpersonal and communication skills, with the ability to engage learners and stakeholders professionally. Ability to identify and address training or learner issues and implement appropriate strategies. Strong organisational skills, with experience in planning training sessions and managing documentation and compliance requirements. Demonstrated teamwork, problem-solving skills, and proficiency in Microsoft Office and relevant digital system s. Benefits On-site gym and fitness programs available. Located in historic Fremantle. Within walking distance from public transport. How to apply Please apply online with a resume and cover letter outlining your suitability for the role. Applications closing date : 10 November 2026 For further information on how to apply, please visit: How to apply | Notre Dame Applicants are expected to have current and valid work rights in Australia. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people are encouraged to apply. The University of Notre Dame Australia seeks to increase the diversity of our workforce to better meet the different needs of the University and its stakeholders and to improve equal opportunity outcomes for our staff. The University is committed to providing a safe, respectful and inclusive working and learning environment. The successful candidate will be required to complete a self-declaration regarding Gender-Based Violence (as required by the National Code) as part of the pre-employment process. For more information on this please refer to our website. Contact us Please direct your queries regarding this role to Marcia Rodrigues , Marcia.Rodrigues@nd.edu.au Unsolicited contact from recruitment agencies will not be entertained. Back to current openings"
    }
  },
